raw:
{
  "description": "As a remote worker based in Portland, I purchased a high-performance ergonomic keyboard and mechanical mouse set via Amazon, totaling $475 on a single receipt. The keyboard is a Logitech G Pro X, and the mouse is a G502 X, both with serial numbers and used for daily development work. The items were delivered in a single package with one invoice, and I’ve attached the receipt. I’ve been using this setup since the beginning of the quarter, and it was not part of my initial equipment stipend. While the total is under $500, the items are hardware with serial numbers and are not consumable. I submitted this under 'equipment' because the purchase was for a durable, non-consumable work tool intended to improve long-term productivity and reduce strain during remote development sessions.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, as specified in the Equipment section of the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy, and the claim does not fall under any higher-precedence routing rule."
}

{
  "description": "As part of the Q3 team-building initiative, I purchased a high-performance dual-monitor setup for the finance_analyst team’s shared workspace to support data visualization and reporting workflows. The total cost of $485 was incurred on a single receipt for a 27-inch 4K monitor, a mechanical keyboard, and a USB-C docking station. The monitor has a serial number and is intended for long-term use in the office; the keyboard and docking station are included as part of the bundled setup. No other items were purchased on this receipt, and the purchase was made using the company’s procurement account. The item is not a consumable, and the total is under $500.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, and although the bundled purchase is a single transaction, the total is below $500, so the $500 threshold does not trigger needs_finance."
}

{
  "description": "As a remote team lead for the West Coast engineering squad, I organized a quarterly team-building event to foster collaboration and morale. The event was held at a private retreat center in Lake Tahoe, and included a full-day workshop, lunch for 8 team members, and a group dinner. For the workshop, I purchased a high-performance dual-monitor setup for the team lead’s home office to support remote collaboration during sprint planning sessions. The total cost of the bundle — a 32-inch 4K monitor, a 27-inch ultrabook display, and a premium ergonomic stand — came to $1,495 on a single receipt, all purchased from a certified vendor. The equipment is intended to replace outdated hardware that’s caused repeated connectivity issues during virtual stand-ups. The team event was a formal company initiative, not a personal gathering, and included all 8 remote engineers who have been working on the same project for over six months. The event was structured around professional development and was documented in the team calendar as a 'remote team alignment summit' with clear OKRs attached. The expense claim includes the full receipt, detailed itemization, and a signed authorization from the department head. The monitors and stand are serial-numbered and will be used exclusively for company business, with no personal use expected.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "The claim involves hardware with a total receipt value over $500, which routes to needs_finance per the Equipment rule in the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy."
}

{
  "description": "Urgent request for approval: I need to purchase a new high-performance laptop for my team lead, who is managing a critical client rollout this quarter. The current device is failing during video calls and crashes during data analysis sessions, severely impacting delivery timelines. I’ve found a Dell XPS 15 with a 32GB RAM upgrade and 1TB SSD, priced at $485, on a verified vendor site with a receipt available. The total is under $500, but it’s a high-impact tool for a client-facing project. This is not a personal purchase — the device will be used exclusively for work and is essential for maintaining client trust and project velocity. The purchase is also necessary because the team lead’s previous machine was officially issued by the company, and we’re not allowed to use personal devices for client engagements. I’m submitting this claim immediately to avoid further delays in the client deliverables.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, and this claim involves a new laptop (equipment) purchased for a work-related purpose, meeting the amount and receipt conditions."
}

{
  "description": "As part of the Q3 client deliverable for the Meridian Health Systems integration, the consulting team required a high-performance workstation upgrade to support real-time data visualization and simulation modeling. I purchased a Dell XPS 17 laptop with a 32GB RAM configuration, 1TB SSD, and a 4K UHD display, along with a premium docking station and a dual-monitor setup, all on a single receipt totaling $1,895. The equipment was acquired to enable seamless collaboration during team events involving remote client stakeholders and is essential for delivering the project on schedule. The purchase was approved internally through the team’s project budget and includes all necessary documentation, including the receipt and a justification note from the project lead confirming the technical requirements.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "The total receipt amount for the bundled hardware purchase exceeds $500, which routes the claim to needs_finance according to the Equipment section of the Expense Software Policy."
}

{
  "description": "As part of a team onboarding event for the new project cohort, I purchased a high-performance 27-inch external monitor for the shared workbench at the Austin office. The monitor is intended to replace an outdated display that is no longer functional and is currently used by the entire team during collaborative sessions. The device was bought from a certified vendor (Dell Direct) on a personal credit card due to urgent delivery needs, and the receipt was issued in USD for $432.80. Although the purchase was made for team use, the monitor is a non-consumable hardware item with a serial number and an original retail price exceeding $400. It was not a bundled purchase, and the item is not eligible for office supplies classification due to its durability and identification features. No company credit card was available at the time, and I submitted the expense within 45 days of the purchase.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, as stated in the Equipment section of the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy, and this claim involves a non-consumable hardware item purchased for team use but below the $500 threshold."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, just submitted a claim for a new ergonomic keyboard and mouse set I bought for my desk setup—$320 total, receipt attached. I'm a finance analyst, and I've been doing a lot of data modeling lately, and my old peripherals were giving me wrist pain. I figured this was a professional upgrade that’d help me be more efficient, especially since I’m working from home 3 days a week. The keyboard is a mechanical one with red switches, and the mouse has a right-handed design with adjustable DPI—both are branded and have serial numbers, so they’re definitely not consumables. I didn’t buy them as part of a bundle, just as a personal investment in my workspace. I know the policy says accessories under $50 can go to office_supplies if they’re consumable, but these are clearly not—they're durable, high-end tools that’ll last years. I’m not claiming it as a software license or anything, just as a new equipment purchase to support my work. No client was involved, and it’s not a team event—just me upgrading my setup to prevent burnout. The claim is under $500, but since it’s hardware with a serial number and over $500? Wait, no—$320, so under $500. But still, it’s not a consumable, so not office_supplies. And it’s not software, even though it’s for my work. I think it should go to needs_manager because it’s under $500 and has a receipt, but I’m not sure if the serial number changes anything. I’ve seen the policy say that anything with a serial number is equipment, regardless of price. So if it’s equipment and under $500, then needs_manager, right?",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, and accessories with serial numbers are classified as equipment regardless of price."
}

{
  "description": "Urgent request for approval: need to replace a broken 27-inch monitor used by the lead developer on the critical Q3 platform rollout. The current monitor has a cracked screen and is causing workflow delays—team has been holding daily stand-ups around it for the past 3 days. I’ve sourced a replacement Dell UltraSharp U2723QE (27-inch 4K monitor, $429) from the company’s preferred vendor; receipt attached. This is not a luxury item—it’s essential for development continuity. The purchase qualifies as equipment under the policy. Since the monitor is being used in a client-facing project and is not a consumable, it cannot be filed under office_supplies. The total is under $500, but the item has a serial number, so it’s clearly equipment per the software policy guidelines. The team event context is relevant because the developer has been working on a 24/7 deployment sprint for the past week, and this monitor is part of the team’s shared physical workspace setup—no personal use involved. Please approve immediately to avoid further delays in the project timeline.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager per the Equipment section of the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy, and this claim involves a monitor with a serial number, which is classified as equipment, not office supplies."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, so I just got back from setting up the new dev environment for the Acme project, and I had to replace my old laptop since the screen cracked during a flight. I picked up a brand-new Dell XPS 15 with the 32GB RAM and 1TB SSD — it was a bit of a splurge, but it’s the only machine that can handle the Docker setup and the full-stack testing suite we need. The receipt got lost in my bag during the layover, so I don’t have it anymore. The total was $678, and I’ve attached the order confirmation and the shipping label from the vendor just in case. I know this is over $500, and I should’ve kept the receipt, but I’m really counting on this machine to keep the project on track — it’s not just for me, it’s for the whole team’s productivity. I’m not trying to game the system, I just dropped the receipt in the trash without thinking when I got off the plane.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware over $500 routes to needs_finance, but missing a receipt moves it to needs_manager, as per the routing policy’s rule that claims without receipts go to needs_manager regardless of amount."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, we just wrapped up the big sprint for the new client portal, and I wanted to throw a proper team celebration to keep morale high. So I booked a full-day offsite at a local retreat center for the entire dev team—11 people total. We did team-building workshops, had lunch catered with a mix of vegetarian and non-vegetarian options, and capped it with dinner and a few non-alcoholic drinks. The total came to $625, and I’ve attached the receipt. The venue included a premium conference room with AV equipment, but the only thing we actually purchased was the food, drinks, and the rental fee for the space. I didn’t get a receipt for the AV setup since it was included in the package, but I did get one for the food and drinks portion ($380), the rental fee ($245), and the catering add-ons. I know this is technically a team event, but since the team is internal and we’re celebrating project milestones, I’m treating it as a team morale event rather than client entertainment. I know the amount is over $500, and the event had more than 3 people involved, but I’m not sure if this counts as a client-related thing or just internal team stuff. Just wanted to flag it for routing.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "The claim is for a team event involving 11 employees, but the expense is categorized as equipment because the rental of a conference room with AV equipment (which includes hardware like projectors and audio systems) is considered equipment under the policy, and the total amount exceeds $500, routing it to needs_finance."
}

{
  "description": "Finance analyst submitted a claim for a $215 dinner at a high-end restaurant in downtown Chicago to discuss the Q3 financial forecast with the Acme Corp client. The meal included a bottle of wine ($78) and two entrees ($137), with the total receipt showing $215. The client was present for the entire duration of the dinner, and the expense was submitted within 45 days of the purchase date. The receipt includes the original currency (USD), and the meal was held at the client's request to strengthen the partnership ahead of a potential contract renewal. The analyst also included a mileage claim of 28 miles from their home office to the restaurant, calculated at the standard IRS rate of $0.67 per mile, totaling $18.76. This mileage is listed separately on the same receipt as a line item, but it was not a reimbursable expense under company policy since it was incidental to a client entertainment event.",
  "category": "client_entertainment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "The claim involves a client dinner with a client present, which qualifies as client_entertainment under the policy; the amount exceeds $150, which triggers routing to needs_finance per the routing policy's rule for client_entertainment over $150."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, I drove my personal car 220 miles this week for a client site visit in downtown Chicago—just the usual commute to the office, but I figured since I was working on a high-priority project for the Acme account, I should submit the mileage since we’re tracking it for tax purposes. The total came to $168, which is under $500, and I’ve got the trip log with timestamps and odometer readings. I’m not claiming any gas or tolls—just the IRS standard mileage rate. But I did use a USB-C to HDMI adapter to connect my laptop to the client’s projector, and I bought that out of pocket since I didn’t have one. It was $37, and it’s got a serial number, so I’m not sure if it’s equipment or office supplies. I’m not trying to game the system—just want to get reimbursed for the work I did and the small item I needed to make the presentation work.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "The $37 USB-C to HDMI adapter has a serial number, which categorizes it as equipment under the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy, and since it’s under $500 and has a receipt, it routes to needs_manager per the standard hardware under $500 rule."
}

{
  "description": "As part of the company's annual remote team-building initiative, I purchased a high-performance 4K monitor (model X27U), a dual-input USB-C docking station, and a noise-canceling headset for my home office setup to enhance collaboration during virtual sprint planning sessions. The total invoice amount was $620.75, and the receipt is attached. This equipment was acquired specifically to support consistent, high-quality video communication and workflow integration across distributed teams, ensuring alignment with the team’s productivity goals. The purchase was made through the company’s preferred vendor, and the devices are intended for long-term use in remote work environments. The bundled receipt includes all three items under a single transaction, and none of the components have serial numbers indicating personal ownership.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "Bundled hardware purchases exceeding $500 in total value are routed to needs_finance, as per the equipment rules in the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy, which applies to receipts with multiple items combined."
}

{
  "description": "Hi, I'm really confused about this claim — I bought a new laptop for my work, but it was on my personal credit card because I needed it urgently for the Q3 deliverables. It's a MacBook Pro 16-inch with 2TB SSD, and the receipt says $1,899.99, but I think the company might consider it equipment? I wasn't sure if I should file it as a one-time software license or something else, but I don't really use software licenses for this. The receipt is attached, and I have a note that it's for work, but I also used it to finish a client proposal last week. I'm just worried because I saw the policy says personal devices get rejected, but I thought this was a work necessity. Also, I noticed that there's a $500 threshold for equipment — this is way over, so I'm assuming it goes to finance? But I don't know if the fact that I used my personal card changes anything. And why is it so hard to figure out if I can get this reimbursed? I just want to make sure I’m not doing it wrong.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "Hardware at or over $500 routes to needs_finance, and this laptop exceeds that threshold, regardless of personal payment or work purpose."
}

{
  "description": "Urgent bulk order of 500 high-capacity printer cartridges and 300 ream packs of premium paper for the entire finance team's quarterly reporting cycle—delivered today, receipt attached, total invoice $68.23, all items are standard office consumables, no serial numbers, all under $50 per unit, and the purchase was made directly through the approved vendor portal. The order was flagged for expedited processing due to critical deadline pressure on the year-end audit, but it's purely for operational office needs, not software or equipment. The finance analyst is requesting immediate approval as the team is already behind schedule.",
  "category": "office_supplies",
  "routing": "auto_approve",
  "rationale": "The claim qualifies for auto_approve because it is under $75, falls under the office_supplies category (consumables like printer ink and paper), and includes a receipt, satisfying all three required conditions."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, so I was working from home last weekend and my personal laptop screen completely died — it just went black during a Zoom call with the finance team. I had to scramble to get a replacement because I couldn’t access any of the client reports or the quarterly forecast. I bought a used Dell XPS 13 on eBay for $620, including the original battery and a new screen. It came with a receipt and a serial number. I know it’s not company equipment, but I was literally doing work for the company on it, and I didn’t have time to wait for procurement. I’m not trying to get reimbursed for personal stuff — I just don’t want to get penalized for doing my job under pressure. Can we make an exception? The laptop’s been running fine since I set it up this morning.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "Hardware at or over $500 routes to needs_finance, and this claim involves a used laptop purchased for work purposes, which falls under the equipment category despite being a personal device."
}

{
  "description": "A dinner meeting was held at Le Petit Bistro in downtown Chicago on April 15, 2024, to discuss the ongoing integration project with the Acme Corporation. The total invoice amount was $225, with a detailed receipt provided. The meal included three entrées, two bottles of wine, and a dessert course. The client’s project manager and two senior engineers from Meridian Consulting were in attendance, and the meeting concluded with a formal agreement on next steps. The expense was submitted on May 23, 2024, which is 38 days after the event. The receipt clearly indicates the presence of a client and includes the original transaction currency in USD.",
  "category": "client_entertainment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "This claim is categorized as client_entertainment because a client was present during the meal; the amount exceeds $150, triggering the needs_finance routing rule per the Expense Approval Routing Policy."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, I just picked up a new Dell XPS 13 for my team’s remote sprint planning — it’s on my personal account for now since we needed it fast, but I’m submitting the receipt so we can reimburse the $1,350 total. I know it’s technically my personal device, but it’s for work, and the company will own it once the license is transferred. The receipt has the serial number, and it’s a full laptop with a built-in keyboard and screen — no peripherals bundled. I didn’t get it through the usual IT channel because the team had to ship it overnight after the last laptop got stolen during a conference. I’m treating this as an urgent equipment replacement, not a personal purchase.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "Hardware at or over $500 routes to needs_finance, and this $1,350 laptop purchase exceeds that threshold despite being initially purchased on a personal account, as it is a company-use device with a serial number and qualifies as equipment under the policy."
}

{
  "description": "As part of a critical project rollout, I acquired a high-performance 27-inch 4K monitor with a built-in USB-C hub and HDR support for remote workstation optimization. The device is intended for long-term use in my role as a senior engineer and was purchased from a certified vendor with a receipt dated the same day. The total cost of the item was $425, which is under the $500 threshold for single-item purchases. However, the monitor has a serial number, is permanently attached to my desk setup, and is not a consumable. While the cost is below $500, the item is clearly hardware with a serial number, and per the software policy, such accessories are classified as equipment and not office supplies. The purchase was made to support team productivity, but the categorization is based on the item itself, not the intended use. Given that the item is non-consumable, has a serial number, and exceeds the $50 threshold for office supplies, it falls under the equipment category. Despite the under-$500 amount, the equipment rule takes precedence over the auto-approval and needs_manager thresholds.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, as per the Equipment section of the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy, which explicitly excludes such items from auto-approval and applies the needs_manager queue."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, I’m really confused about this claim — I had a dinner with the Acme client last night to go over the final deliverables for their project, and I thought it was just a standard business meeting. I ended up spending $195 on the meal, which included a bottle of wine and a few appetizers, but I wasn’t sure if the wine part should be separated since the receipt just shows a total. I’m not even sure why I’m getting flagged — I attached the receipt, it was a client dinner, and I didn’t think it was over the limit. I was told it’s going to needs_finance, but I thought under $500 it should just go to my manager? I mean, the client was there, so it’s not just a team lunch, right? And I didn’t do anything wrong — I even double-checked the policy. But I can’t figure out why this is getting escalated so high. I just want to know if this is normal or if I’m missing something. Also, I’m working remotely, so I’m not sure if that changes anything — I’m not traveling or anything. I just met the client at a restaurant in my city. It’s all so confusing.",
  "category": "client_entertainment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "Any client_entertainment over $150 routes to needs_finance per the routing policy."
}

{
  "description": "Bought a new MacBook Pro for my personal use during a weekend trip to Portland — it’s not for work, but I needed a reliable machine to prep for the team sync next week. The receipt is attached, and the total was $1,750. I know it’s not company property, but I figured the claim might still count since I’m using it for work purposes now.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "reject",
  "rationale": "Personal items, including personal electronics, are rejected per the routing policy, even if claimed for work-related reasons."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, so I had to replace my old laptop because it died during a client demo last week—kinda embarrassing when your screen goes black mid-presentation. I got a new one on sale, $720 total, and I used my personal card since the company one was maxed out. The receipt is missing though—I dropped the printer copy in the trash by accident. It was a Dell XPS 15 with a 16GB RAM and 512GB SSD, and the receipt was just a paper slip from the store that I never got a digital copy of. I know it’s not a company device, but I needed it for work, and I’ve been using it for all my client reports and travel prep. Can we still get this approved? I promise I’ll be more careful with receipts next time.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, but this hardware is over $500 and the receipt is missing, so it defaults to needs_manager per the missing receipt rule in the routing policy."
}

{
  "description": "Spent $315 on a dinner reservation at Le Jardin, a high-end French bistro in downtown Chicago, for a client meeting with the Acme Corp. team. The invoice lists three main courses, one bottle of wine (chardonnay), and a dessert for each of the four attendees, including myself and three senior engineers. The total includes a 20% service charge, and the receipt clearly states that the client was present and actively engaged throughout the evening. The expense was incurred on a Monday evening, and the receipt is attached, showing the original amount in USD. There are no other associated charges, and no personal items were purchased. This was a formal client engagement aimed at securing a follow-up project, and the client has since sent a thank-you email confirming the meeting’s positive outcome.",
  "category": "client_entertainment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "The claim involves client entertainment with a client present and exceeds $150, which routes to needs_finance per the routing policy."
}

