{
  "description": "Just wanted to follow up on the client dinner we had last week—got the receipt from the new upscale bistro downtown, Le Bistro du Monde, where we met with the Acme Corp team for dinner. It was a 4-person table, and I ordered the steak frites with a glass of red wine, but the server accidentally charged me for a bottle of premium cabernet that I didn’t actually consume—so the total came to $147, including tax. I didn’t realize until after the fact, but I did bring my personal credit card to cover it since the company card was tied up with the travel booking. It was meant to be a professional catch-up, and the client did attend, so it was definitely for business. That said, I know the wine was a bit of a stretch—definitely not the only thing on the receipt, but I figured I’d just claim the meal part since it was a client dinner. Still, I feel weird about using my personal card, but hey, it’s just a $147 dinner and it was for a real client meeting.",
  "category": "client_entertainment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "The claim involves client_entertainment where a client was present and the amount exceeds $150, which routes to needs_finance per the routing policy."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, I just picked up a new 27-inch ultra-wide monitor for my home office setup since the old one died during a sprint review last week. It was $480 on sale, and I got the receipt from the electronics store. It’s a high-res display with built-in speakers and USB-C passthrough, so I can plug in my laptop and charge it at the same time. I know it’s under $500, but I figured it’s essential for doing design work and video calls with the team, especially since I’m managing the new product rollout. I’ve been using my personal monitor for the past two weeks and it’s been a nightmare with the resolution, so this was a necessary upgrade to keep things professional. Let me know if you need any additional details — I can send a photo of the receipt or the product specs if needed.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_manager",
  "rationale": "Hardware under $500 with a receipt routes to needs_manager, as per the Equipment section of the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy."
}

{
  "description": "Hey, so I bought this new high-end gaming laptop for my personal use—like, I know it's not company stuff, but it's a 17-inch 4K display with an RTX 4090 and a custom mechanical keyboard, and I kind of need it for side projects I'm working on outside of work. It's not for work, obviously, but I figured since I'm doing freelance consulting stuff on it, and the invoice came from a legit business account, maybe it counts? Anyway, it was $1,800 total on one receipt, and I've got the receipt and all the details. I know it's way over $500, and it's definitely equipment, not office supplies. But it's technically for personal use—no company branding, no work-related software on it, just games and some personal apps. Still, I wanted to submit it in case there’s a loophole or if it qualifies under some edge case? Like, could it be considered a 'personal item' even though it's over $500? I’m not sure, but I figured I’d put it in for review since it's not really work equipment and I wouldn’t want to get in trouble if it’s rejected. Anyway, just thought I’d flag it.",
  "category": "equipment",
  "routing": "needs_finance",
  "rationale": "Hardware at or over $500 routes to needs_finance, regardless of personal use, as stated in the Equipment section of the Software & Equipment Purchasing Policy."
